# Round 49 — Siegel zeros and zero repulsion: only near s = 1

| item | status 2026 | relevance to off-line zeros of ζ |
|------|-------------|----------------------------------|
| Zhang 2022 (Landau–Siegel) | serious gap found; not accepted | none |
| Deuring–Heilbronn repulsion | established (conditional on an exceptional zero) | near 1 only |
| Conrey–Iwaniec (Siegel zeros ⇒ small gaps) | conditional | spacing ≠ real parts |
| Granville–Stark (abc), Goldfeld–Gross–Zagier | conditional / effective class numbers | real Dirichlet zeros only |
| Heath-Brown (Siegel zeros ⇒ twin primes) | conditional | none |
| small gaps, simple zeros, Lehmer pairs | active | on-line zeros only |
| Iwaniec–Sarnak central values | conditional | near 1 only |
| repulsion analogue for ζ | no local displacement theorem | finite-height test at best |

Zero repulsion is the only "one zero constrains another" mechanism known, and it works through the pole
at s = 1 (cf. R30, R31): its reach is again O(1/log t).
